<div class="unit-details">

    <!-- Overview & Objectives Split -->
    <div class="grid-2-col">
        <div class="content-box">
            <h2 class="content-title"><i class="fa-solid fa-binoculars"></i> Unit Overview</h2>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.5;">This unit helps you understand how modern apps are
                designed to keep people watching, scrolling, or playing — and how to build healthy digital
                habits. You will look at real examples from the apps you use every day: notifications, endless
                scroll, autoplay, streaks, pop-ups, and recommended content. You will learn simple thinking
                tools (spotting patterns, simplifying ideas, breaking routines into steps) to make sense of how
                these designs work. The unit focuses on balance, attention, and wellbeing. You will create a
                short, clear "Digital Balance Guide" using easy, practical tips you can actually follow.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="content-box">
            <h2 class="content-title"><i class="fa-solid fa-bullseye"></i> Unit Objectives</h2>
            <ul class="custom-list">
                <li>Recognise common app features that are designed to grab attention.</li>
                <li>Explain how notifications, scrolling, colours, and sounds influence behaviour.</li>
                <li>Spot patterns in their own tech habits and identify what helps or distracts them.</li>
                <li>Break down one digital habit into steps to see where change is possible.</li>
                <li>Understand why balance matters for focus, sleep, and wellbeing.</li>
                <li>Produce a simple, helpful "Digital Balance Guide" that uses clear ideas and good judgement.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Concepts -->
    <div class="content-box">
        <h2 class="content-title"><i class="fa-solid fa-lightbulb"></i> Concepts</h2>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">This unit focuses on four SGS Computer Science concepts:</p>
        <div class="concepts-grid">
            <div class="concept-card">
                <h3 class="concept-name"><i class="fa-solid fa-cogs"></i> Function</h3>
                <p class="concept-def">What something is designed to do.</p>
                <p class="concept-context"><strong>In this unit:</strong> Students look at why apps use features
                    like notifications, streaks, and autoplay.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="concept-card">
                <h3 class="concept-name"><i class="fa-solid fa-fingerprint"></i> Adaptation</h3>
                <p class="concept-def">Adjusting behaviour or habits.</p>
                <p class="concept-context"><strong>In this unit:</strong> Students identify small changes they
                    can make to stay in control of their tech use.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="concept-card">
                <h3 class="concept-name"><i class="fa-solid fa-shapes"></i> Form</h3>
                <p class="concept-def">How information is organised.</p>
                <p class="concept-context"><strong>In this unit:</strong> Students simplify their wellbeing
                    advice so it's easy to understand and follow.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="concept-card">
                <h3 class="concept-name"><i class="fa-solid fa-diagram-project"></i> Logic</h3>
                <p class="concept-def">Rules behind behaviour.</p>
                <p class="concept-context"><strong>In this unit:</strong> Students notice patterns in how apps
                    behave and how users respond.</p>
            </div>
